#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054571 :question/id #uuid "92c02155-ad64-59a7-be4c-052e5bdfb429" :question/math-academy-id "e-6096" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f87e0eb91b6ec47ca8a4d64c2824396e613123ed37862975b606608ff70445f0.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=5-2x$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5), B(2,1)$ and $C(3,0)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a true statement:\n$y|= 5 - 2x; (5)|= 5 - 2(0); 5|= 5✓$\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line:\n- Substituting the point $B(2,1)$ results in a true statement:\n$y|= 5 - 2x; (1)|= 5 - 2(2); 1|= 1✓$\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line:\n- Substituting the point $C(3,0)$ results in a false statement:\n$y|= 5 - 2x; (0)|= 5 - 2(3); 0|=-1\\cdot$\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line:"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4e7efc6-2907-5146-bf87-e5dadaa7ef87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A, B$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052119} {:answer/id #uuid "113d6841-6789-509b-8adf-0487e9a14aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052120} {:answer/id #uuid "25076717-a6b6-5e26-b5d4-9eeb494aa9c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052121} {:answer/id #uuid "7172bb65-a488-55f3-85a5-2a897483409f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052122} {:answer/id #uuid "798a3ace-bf62-53c2-b076-4cb83ba3fcf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052123}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d5226921-bc7e-5b2a-be0c-3dc1f726fdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052118}] :question/id #uuid "591488e6-be15-5117-89a8-9b785af2738a" :question/math-academy-id "q-22405" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c63af2b4cd9209077baee74e5f4f4059bc1c8c7e4b2cdc620c872d025fe5a4e9.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y = x + 1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dab0521-43ce-56d3-9766-bfd063a26a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053014} {:answer/id #uuid "40308272-8e0a-57e0-960c-1a0f39a7b30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053015} {:answer/id #uuid "4574c630-7a0a-5a36-b9f0-676f7f3337ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053016} {:answer/id #uuid "5017fd9c-f6fa-58f6-a71b-bdcc271bb28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053017} {:answer/id #uuid "7f341a43-f964-56e9-af66-69149d8f802e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053018}] :answer-field/id #uuid "441ead36-9592-50bd-9122-72ce99851742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053013}] :question/id #uuid "bfa79719-c636-549a-8b52-62d1b8be1bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-44504" :question/problem "![](images/20/20e05c0af32d6fe2643af2912d51ef1b3994374260ffaa4fc22002def474c15f.png)\n\nIdentify the points on the graph that lie on the line $y = 8x - 3$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054602 :question/id #uuid "6b16ff95-8b78-587d-a7c8-8d0909b69f19" :question/math-academy-id "e-983" :question/problem "Use the table below to plot the graph of $y=3x-2$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$, we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-2$.\nTherefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-2=-2$,\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-2=1$,\n- if $x=2$ then $y=3(2)-2=4$, and\n- if $x=3$ then $y=3(3)-2=7$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/09/09e670353b2114b229769e2bd6b3eb4a9895389b818fc3f4ace6997a3e7efa3f.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=3x-2$.\n\n![](images/d2/d2d8891102ce99020f544bd8d82a5ff0454080474ce4267ef5285db92f5b2ca0.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2491cf6c-260b-5992-a910-9fe6a80e30bc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95871ea8-427c-5d8b-b7b9-90621fa03a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a26ee2942f083a2edb99e9839d71577e1772bf6410e1ae4beafcde7f59c7109.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053458} {:answer/id #uuid "c7240b34-1c9b-5ca5-9452-e210e669f37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/893dba533e0afffe05f9603293355ccf38b2723fcd91608ea03fe321e26a0d5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053459} {:answer/id #uuid "17588e8d-da64-5d6b-ab34-45af1c4a7804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/eaf735f72849b82e92f7baf0d991f3e024e013b66d9813d4e0d5f3a87632c901.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053460} {:answer/id #uuid "27023196-b279-5485-8ce7-7bf4dc0c7ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/afb9d602e1bff2fb643f44ed5dfa09d41833740e814739c60d5dd890a2f02270.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053461} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8ce376-c015-5edf-afdd-204f6ccb159f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/02f0d3d8bb1bd1e87d05e63579e2a92281de1d000fe2baa7c9942dd80b542efb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053462}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbe1741-021b-5eb6-8d9e-91ce5412b307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053457}] :question/id #uuid "4524558d-8a83-5843-a336-69f66463c3a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-54397" :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y = x - 2$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8012f237-f895-59fa-8270-6503cec53a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33840084a698d261095927ca6ca12e85673665e3dc00264e1d21722a60c16fa4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053812} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce4d676-af93-589c-ba26-6402379e878e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/893dba533e0afffe05f9603293355ccf38b2723fcd91608ea03fe321e26a0d5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053813} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b7f4ba-210d-5ab9-a5f8-a2904e7fedf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/416bc4e441bac223991d615915c282fed3ecae8ececd4ce04433a1eacfad47f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053814} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e4e655-149e-5749-b055-169473ebdae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e1e94d8d56f09e4c5d2b47cbd04ec99d51f97465d8144683f16a5689fd6324c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053815} {:answer/id #uuid "40c8bec8-cf09-50fd-97ef-11e1dc5e4980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/eaf735f72849b82e92f7baf0d991f3e024e013b66d9813d4e0d5f3a87632c901.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053816}] :answer-field/id #uuid "423e8e65-dd75-5e41-82c6-09a914060bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053811}] :question/id #uuid "4cd916a5-f5b2-585b-bea7-8dda3f5e10d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-59676" :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y =-x + 1$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Line Using a Table"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054603 :question/id #uuid "4aad1578-4134-5f25-88cb-8ab4631257ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-984" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3$." :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\nIt is often easiest to pick one of the points to have an $x$-coordinate of $x=0$. So, for the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0$. Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(0) + 3 \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,3)$.\n\nFor the second point, let's choose $x=4$. Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(4) + 3 \\\\\n&=-2 + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(4,1)$.\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3\n$$\n\n![](images/36/36689081acaacb24a09826c3927388697e77d92caeca900d053d33d0f99d2b8c.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebeb4e5a-adbe-5a94-b05d-61933a687892" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0a3946e-4d98-5306-b4b2-f900a60e9abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff2f94655de95a7a5099423b3d0dbbb9bb6e64f39aca105eb8cab1366f547064.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053452} {:answer/id #uuid "f18a5b0b-4150-51bf-ae4a-e1621ffc7785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bc5f7358456a111f29f900e8148e60a0fede85ca9b951e3d752ca1e1a800e6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053453} {:answer/id #uuid "15e16ea3-e05e-53e9-8ea7-ae8c5c48c14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7ed9a41dc38df65a985785c318e5d0407a43615cef7dfa0c60ad454099a089a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053454} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb80dc2-f93b-5a21-82fe-a63bca5f9b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0cfc4bba4867b40c4c38b0886c37bb5c4cd64d293530ece7c67cdb6349134013.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053455} {:answer/id #uuid "3f60c829-2580-5c06-aab1-c1c58924a888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/0678cd88949fac3a9ce58d255eee9d8a07ba8c3b91a86f90fa24d70d1510568f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053456}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b4ae38-d4b8-5d3e-8141-6ba4d4385b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053451}] :question/id #uuid "28feb85a-a0a4-55cd-b26b-1f144b97ee7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54286" :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y = 2x - 4$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b72f52d3-7c21-5555-bf79-a5e32ea63dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e2977ee1faa5dde2d1bf3a427037aac75b2692486bdcb724356452765c8b661.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053818} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a6f114-0b92-53a7-be81-3901b4da5d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0cfc4bba4867b40c4c38b0886c37bb5c4cd64d293530ece7c67cdb6349134013.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053819} {:answer/id #uuid "eddea0ed-d911-5e32-b099-869db5076ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/45177c5e17c0040286e8404613e88f5f472417ae3a640ad7fb5fa6468646fedd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053820} {:answer/id #uuid "09a91346-d838-5250-81eb-a1248c4d8558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c7577f7033f36315d50748424d7a8db635e9aecd4c88c9ed4626f3294bc6c2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053821} {:answer/id #uuid "11cb7f7a-bdd2-52bd-aa97-ada840a3b84a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff2f94655de95a7a5099423b3d0dbbb9bb6e64f39aca105eb8cab1366f547064.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053822}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d1196c-1fdd-5a78-9a03-811c69c20b33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053817}] :question/id #uuid "f8b0e058-eb84-5845-9c9e-ed354a3b82f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-59678" :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y = \\frac{1}{2}x - 2$?"}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054499 :question/id #uuid "2efe9f75-18c0-5a86-b56b-3f338b7e5c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1702" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the graph of the straight line below?\n\n1. $y=2x-1$\n2. $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$\n3. $2x-y=1$\n\n![](images/93/93dc9d447c15e4597549996b1f0b533cf86783b5cc5df032a4c46d4ca8934abc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,-1)$ and $(2,3)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/a9/a9f18453dedb1763908825301f80ef86307d3a3aceaf678bbe8579a42a0996f5.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=2x-1$.\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3)$, we obtain\n$(-1)|= 2(0) - 1; - 1|=-1✓; (3)|= 2(2) - 1; 3|= 3✓$\nTherefore, $y=2x-1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n- Equation II: $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$.\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3)$, we obtain\n$(-1)|= \\frac{1}{2}(0) - 1; - 1|=-1✓; (3)|= \\frac{1}{2}(2) - 1; 3|= 0\\cdot$\nTherefore, $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x-1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $2x-y=1$.\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,-1)$ and $B(2,3)$, we obtain\n$2(0) - (-1)|= 1; 1|= 1✓; 2(2) - (3)|= 1; 1|= 1✓$\nTherefore, $2x-y=1$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that represented the line in the graph were $y=2x-1$ and $2x-y=1$, corresponding to answer choices I and III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3986a3cd-c543-54e9-8d55-f744d9436313" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86184d72-71eb-5266-97aa-98ca9333aac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053446} {:answer/id #uuid "f56f9fc1-a41f-52d1-a174-4f44b3a36e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053447} {:answer/id #uuid "38764fde-baad-5551-b71a-bb9e399ca769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053448} {:answer/id #uuid "5732fc71-f59d-5bbd-a6fc-3a658071237e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053449} {:answer/id #uuid "70ba90c3-8f12-5e6e-8510-a650716f0851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053450}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7beff78d-9b7e-5a87-a424-6ec7aa9ddf98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053445}] :question/id #uuid "56e71e28-d3a5-545f-bf8d-30361fbf20ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-54285" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4ce176ba6bb73da8d6af410bedad4733cf3e705c70e28f133d771b89f78130c5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\n1. $y = x + \\frac{1}{2}$\n2. $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2$\n3. $y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c180d864-bc07-583b-a883-481866f09213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053824} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1c6236-4f98-5028-9df1-1a3d4feea252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053825} {:answer/id #uuid "178a3569-9994-51b1-aeed-54d1afd7d5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053826} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe2b615-e7df-5abb-a7ae-9fb5ebf64f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053827} {:answer/id #uuid "46f3ee86-cbf9-507f-944a-092d5e0e2256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053828}] :answer-field/id #uuid "31eb51f0-5d36-5ca2-a829-8a5c0c692be7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053823}] :question/id #uuid "2fe51fc5-fce7-5fb6-911c-f561e72ff3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-59732" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d78eaaedd26a27d9163fdcddfce5c5ed429e441a75d361f0840935fce9235c6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\n1. $x + 2y = 4$\n2. $y = 2 - x$\n3. $y = 4 - x$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Line Given a Graph"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054574 :question/id #uuid "ae9cba85-b0ed-59c9-bf9f-682b921c5ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6258" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e897cc-b3fe-5d2f-8b47-3afdfe276bcf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "982b97fb-6a3a-5c8c-8476-20fcf0f687c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052406}] :question/id #uuid "6b1e280b-fba4-5891-88fc-5a7c6d06c02c" :question/math-academy-id "q-270966" :question/problem "The polynomial\n$-5x^{4} + 8x^{3} + 29x^{2} - 20x - 12$\ncan be expressed as\n$- (x - 1)(x - 3) \\cdot P(x)$.\nFind the polynomial $P(x)$.$P(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73fb732-c827-51b5-97ad-ba12f00a8502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054154} {:answer/id #uuid "121b62e6-58c9-5cd8-a11f-9637a651f542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - x)(x - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054155} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0578df-e0b2-549e-840c-a126b907d2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054156} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdb57bd-1a8e-588c-9eb3-8aed53bf62e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1)(4 - x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054157} {:answer/id #uuid "7b66aa41-7b18-5a83-bc92-114e28a181aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1)(x - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054158}] :answer-field/id #uuid "41b9e11e-b5e8-5bb4-9b08-05f9cb0de09d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054153}] :question/id #uuid "6573d8e0-c3bf-5172-9e20-d7cf0a1fe1d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75709" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$x^{4} - 3x^{3} - 8x^{2} + 12x + 16 = (x + 1)(x - 4)P(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf5ef13-6f98-5504-9321-69efb2812449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x + 1)(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054160} {:answer/id #uuid "ba25cfb0-f065-5263-ac0b-3cd479db45e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x - 1)(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054161} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdf00c9-062b-5a8d-b75f-d38accd1f3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x + 1)(x - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054162} {:answer/id #uuid "26270474-df68-5557-ac6a-cc9f0c24450b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x - 1)(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054163} {:answer/id #uuid "6696bf5b-31f0-57db-b17b-ea895dec1b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x - 1)(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054164}] :answer-field/id #uuid "83339d20-4175-56fc-a458-b60a76bd3194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054159}] :question/id #uuid "58782aa2-6fd6-5604-b142-52a388630059" :question/math-academy-id "q-75710" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$2x^{4} + 3x^{3} - 4x^{2} - 3x + 2 = (x - 1)(x + 2)P(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140a15bc-fcf1-5cd5-bb3c-e84afc03fb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x-1)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244496} {:answer/id #uuid "29f5e660-86ea-5256-8c05-d4e7429d46d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244687} {:answer/id #uuid "4978aa35-7d7f-5b59-8af9-843564a32b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+1)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244967} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5f3ae5-6893-5186-b94f-2e8294ab9c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-1)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245994} {:answer/id #uuid "bce4f31e-2daa-5469-ae29-c4736cf33aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce4f31e-2daa-5469-ae29-c4736cf33aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5d1fe0-66e0-5072-aedc-5e9a7b18459a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "904dff05-ecb6-51b3-917d-26bcf8537c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-112615" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-13$ | $7$ | $17$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-12$ | $-15$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-7$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2}{x+1}=3{x}^{2}-7x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)(3{x}^{2}-7x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1){{(3x-1)(x-2)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-1)(x-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b24578e-481e-553a-a83a-dc64b6635d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244416} {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb12f3-3780-53db-adc2-7c2e38923858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245004} {:answer/id #uuid "b902a4ce-fd1e-5700-be25-6073ff3196a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245966} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb9253d-1f88-5f8c-9b58-b795ed407dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x-3)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae9a65-2c31-50dc-9b0c-6b6e3de50ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4055097-e27e-5747-8711-3ff850d11a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-112617" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $4$ | $-37$ | $-14$ | $24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $39$ | $6$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8}{x+4}=3{x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4){{(3x-2)(x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-2)(x+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292959fc-5935-574c-9f04-13e8671cf522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-7)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244674} {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} {:answer/id #uuid "45503d60-7034-5dc8-94fc-0693fe24418a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244928} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbc7b91-18d2-5c35-b07b-b9f5fa63305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-x)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246129} {:answer/id #uuid "f620a82c-3ecf-57d2-9154-b09ad9b1016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c5333b-8831-529d-9a73-a9a967c8862b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c8eab9f-7b10-5db7-a80e-51f13f149526" :question/math-academy-id "q-112626" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35.$ We know that $(x-5)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\\\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-36$ | $-2$ | $35$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $35$ | $-5$ | $-35$ |\n|  | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35}{x-5}={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}{x+1}={x}^{2}+6x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}+6x-7).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1){{(x-1)(x+7)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x-1)(x+7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18490f60-4edc-508c-abe1-e7e0f13c065d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285003} {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} {:answer/id #uuid "e405d079-ad9d-55f2-b5eb-d4d63e4f65f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286871} {:answer/id #uuid "eb434cbb-a7e4-590f-89bc-63a9f59cf077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286942} {:answer/id #uuid "f66903c6-f94c-504b-bfea-93036d198d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecf3453-145b-5f3a-bf2b-36b631e0d173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a78f6b62-d8f4-5963-b2aa-dcc684d0394b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18862" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-11$ | $9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-2)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $8$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}+4x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3){{(x+1)(x+3)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x+1)(x+3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039} :answer-field/id #uuid "65363172-90a7-5c3c-8285-eb6ba0e5a62c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c8e3cf5-7e90-5470-a693-ef40ffd528ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-328505" :question/problem "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054575 :question/id #uuid "7e2e4745-f999-5b5e-bf4b-22b9596c384c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6259" :question/problem "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c87ea4-2431-5088-8717-815efd1403ff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc16b9ab-aa9b-5770-a938-e9508ad1fce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052774} {:answer/id #uuid "f80d4806-1ba2-5afa-81f7-c44f279b170f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052775} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb2d533-72c3-568b-ad36-82015aa55185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052776} {:answer/id #uuid "191c4a6c-e51b-58a8-9229-d9c5fa9e1769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052777} {:answer/id #uuid "5acaac2a-7f38-56e0-8094-a0f037c9a0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052778}] :answer-field/id